 want to learn online the idea that  somehow the screen time will harm them  or that they won't be appropriately  socialized I think is really misguided  what we're hoping to socialize these  kids to as I think in a lot of cases  white collar jobs like the kind that  most of us are lucky enough to have to  you know to put them into schools that  are structured around the kind of  industrial you know factory worker jobs  that don't even exist anymore  certainly doesn't do them a service and  so now I just like to talk briefly about  the flip end of online education which  is at the  university-level I suspect many of you  already know more about that it's  certainly been in the news a lot the  massively open online courses or the  hilarious hilarious acronym MOOCs are  pretty big these days Coursera and  Udacity or two of the biggest names in  EDX those are coming out of Stanford and  MIT and Harvard and you also probably  know Khan Academy which is aimed mostly  at high achieving high school students  it gives them tutorials of just all  manner of stuff that they might have  trouble picking up in class there's also  little guys who I think are showing a  lot of promise - including George  Mason's Tyler Cowen and Alex Tabarrok  launched marginal revolution university  which is named after their blog which  some of you may read and what they do is  they just have one course right now and  it's just the two of them teaching this  one course because that's what they're  good at and offering it up the problem  right now was online ed for college is  that there's no iTunes of online ed and  there's no Yelp of online ed so it's  hard to know what's good and it's hard  to know what in for employers to know  what's good once people come out of the  far end this is not something that I am  the first person to notice a lot of  these people are trying really really  hard to figure out how to solve this  problem and just in the past few days  actually there's been some interesting  developments including San Jose State  has started offering some of their  remedial courses through Udacity they're  using online courses to teach remedial  statistics and some other things they  cost about $150 a pop which is  significantly cheaper than the credits  that the school is offering in the  traditional environment needless to say  the professors at San Jose State are not  super thrilled about this development  they shouldn't be they should be nervous  they are about to be replaced and I  think too often in the debate about  online education people are very careful  to say oh there's always room for  teachers teachers or you know they're  apple pie and flags and we just you know  we have to give them everything they  want forever and ever there will always  be room for really good teachers there  will also always be room for good  coaches and good mentors and good  counselors but those people are going to  there  who look very very different and you  know I hope that their pay scales will  also look very very different in the in  the near future there's also been  another development which is at the  University of Wisconsin has started  offering say they're gonna start  offering degrees to people who complete  their coursework elsewhere and they'll  just have to take proficiency tests so  this is something that you know we've  gotten pretty far from the idea that you  go to college to learn a set of stuff  and then employers will know you learn  that stuff because you have a degree  this is the chance to kind of get back  to that it's just one of the functions  of college and and like the very best  teachers I think some of the very top  colleges are fine they can do whatever  they want they can keep doing exactly  what they've been doing you know there's  there will always be a demand for a  Harvard degree but you know the San Jose  states of the world better hustle cuz  they are gonna get put out of business  just very quickly I'd like to talk about  policy and of course it's for those of  you supporting your yellow scarves it's  it's school choice week reason is having  a series of events we had one in DC last  week or earlier this week we've got this  event and there's another one in LA  coming up but you can ask Amy about  school choice has the idea of school  choice has definitely evolved and  expanded and no longer just sort of  means the original Milton Friedman  vision of vouchers and charters but also  also now these new online tools so I'm  very excited to kind of be talking about  this in that context today there are  some policy barriers which you might not  be shocked to hear - - adopting some of  these reforms one of the biggest is  actually one of the most obvious for K  through 12 which is seat time  requirements many many states have  requirements the kids have to have their  butts in chairs within the view of a  teacher for X number of hours every week  that doesn't make any sense but of  course the reasons for those rules are  obvious this is a great job security  measure for teachers unions it's a great  way to make sure that  even when there are other budgetary  pressures that teachers jobs remain  protected there's also the problem of  September to June school year for  anybody who has ever you know moved in  the middle of the year you know this is  like a classic child trauma saying that  you have to you know do third grade over  because your new district doesn't  recognize the credits from your old  district and that's a huge possible  benefit of these online courses is that  they could be interoperable across many  many different systems and in fact you  could continue education completely  uninterrupted even if your parents have  to move for a job there's also the  possible benefit of people being able to  stay in urban areas I live in Washington  DC and I have a young child things don't  look that good for school if I want to  send her to public school and this is  why people you know creative class  workers who would prefer to stay in  cities wind up having to leave it's it's  you know it's not good for those cities  it's not good for the workers it's maybe  not even good for the kids who are  missing out on the kind of benefits of  living in urban areas which they're  simply not possible because of because  of the quality of public schools in  those areas and teacher-to-student  ratios or another problem they don't  make any sense in a world where you know  some of the teaching is done by a  physical teacher who has full  credentials some of the teaching is done  by someone who's an assistant some of  the teaching could be done by somebody  in India who's in your headset saying  hey why don't we try again on that math  problem you know that's I know you can  do it right you can say that from India  it's no problem  and the person's name will be like bulb  it'll be great  but you know there's the biggest problem  and the biggest barrier here is just the  huge political risk to people who want  to innovate in this area there are  people who are dying to get into online  education in fact there are people in  this room who I've talked to over the  last day or so who are dying to get into  online education we've got folks  designing financial literacy courses  we've got people who want to help out  their their friends and neighbors who  are looking for a ways to to improve  their own kids lot but as long as  at the whim of the machine it's not  worth it to invest a ton of money it's  not worth it to invest a ton of time  it's not worth it to stake your  professional future on something that  could be wiped out by you know an  obscure vote in the state legislature in  the middle of the night by 10 guys who  were bought off by teachers unions which  is what happens all the time I mean I'm  not exaggerating when I say that you  know charter schools are often an inn  for online education but there are many  states now where you can't enroll in a  charter even if you have statewide  choice you can't enroll in a charter  that's online focused unless you live in  the district where the Charter is hosted  I don't think they're totally clear on  how the internet works in that case  because the internet costs all the  districts so you know and it's all about  money it's all about funding so I don't  want to end on a on a kind of  pessimistic note so I'm going to end on  a quasi optimistic note which is that I  don't know if we can you know ultimately  reform institutions so that the kind of  blended learning model that I've  described comes to public schools comes  to traditional public schools but I do  know that it's a lot easier to opt out  for people who have been daunted by the  idea of homeschooling for people who are  daunted by the idea of the price of a  private school you know charters and  vouchers have already made this much  more of a real possibility for families  who would otherwise have just been stuck  with you know real estate as destiny for  their schools but I think we're just  gonna see more and more of that you know  you can you can envision all kinds of  things including the flipped school day  where kids consume their lectures at  night and just hang out with tutors  during the day you can imagine vast  warehouses holding kindergarteners or  18-year olds who just want to get out of  the house but would rather you know take  their courses online  you know for-profit providers will will  step up here if if the other components  are there so I don't know if we can fix  it  yet although I do think we can fix it  eventually but I do know that there are  a lot of ways to choose not to be a part  of the dysfunctional school system that  we have right now  and they're increasingly online
 

